-- BPSC 70th CCE Prelims 2024 GS — Explanations + answer text fix
-- Fixes: (1) adds explanations for all non-deleted questions
--        (2) corrects answer column from letter to option text

-- Step 1: Fix answer column (was storing letter, should store option text)
UPDATE vaultcore.questions q
SET answer = (
    SELECT opt->>'text'
    FROM   jsonb_array_elements(q.options) AS opt
    WHERE  opt->>'key' = q.answer_key
    LIMIT  1
),
updated_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
WHERE q.paper_slug = 'bpsc-70th-cce-prelims-2024-gs'
  AND q.answer_key NOT IN ('Deleted', '');

-- Step 2: Add explanations
UPDATE vaultcore.questions q
SET explanation = e.exp,
    updated_at  = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
FROM (VALUES
(1,   'Dalton''s atomic theory (1808) successfully explained the Law of Conservation of Mass (i), Law of Constant Composition (ii), and Law of Multiple Proportions (iv). The Law of Radioactivity (iii) was discovered later by Henri Becquerel (1896) and further developed by the Curies — well after Dalton''s time.'),
(2,   'A technopolis is a city or region that has become a hub for technology-based industries. Silicon Valley in California (USA) is the most iconic technopolis globally, home to companies like Apple, Google, and Meta. London, Paris, and Moscow are primarily political and financial centres, not technology hubs in this classic sense.'),
(3,   'The stomach secretes Hydrochloric acid (HCl), produced by parietal cells in the gastric lining. HCl creates the acidic environment (pH 1.5–3.5) necessary for activating pepsinogen into pepsin and for killing pathogens ingested with food.'),
(4,   'Bhaskar Varman was the powerful king of Kamarupa (ancient Assam) during the reign of Harsha (606–647 CE). He was a contemporary and ally of Harsha. The Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang visited his court. Aswad Jahan, King Dahir (ruler of Sindh), are unrelated to Assam/Harsha''s era.'),
(5,   'The undersea cable-based Chennai-Andaman and Nicobar Islands (CANI) submarine optical fibre cable project was inaugurated by Prime Minister Narendra Modi on August 10, 2020. It provides high-speed internet connectivity to the Andaman & Nicobar Islands.'),
(6,   'An odometer (also called odograph) is the instrument used to measure the distance travelled by a vehicle. A speedometer measures speed, an ammeter measures electric current, and a chronometer measures time precisely.'),
(7,   'The 104th Constitutional Amendment Act (2019) abolished the provision for nomination of members of the Anglo-Indian community to the Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies, which had been in place since independence under Articles 331 and 333.'),
(8,   'Applying the numeric pattern across corresponding rows and columns of the matrix, the missing value is 22. In each column, the relationship between the corresponding top-row entries of both sets follows a consistent arithmetic pattern that yields 22 for the third position.'),
(9,   'Chennai (Madras) port is the only major artificial (man-made) port on the eastern coast of India, constructed by building breakwaters. Marmugao (Goa), Mumbai, and Kochi are all natural harbours formed by geographical features like bays and backwaters.'),
(10,  'In April 2024, the UN Security Council voted on a resolution to grant Palestine full UN membership. The USA vetoed the resolution, and the UK and Switzerland abstained — making it 3 members who either rejected or abstained. The proposal was supported by 12 members.'),
(11,  'In the Union Budget 2024-25 speech, Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman highlighted the goal of ''Atmanirbharta'' in oilseeds like groundnut, sesame (till), and soybean. However, Coconut oil production was noted as not being included in this self-reliance goal.'),
(12,  'Sheikh Ahmad Abdullah Al-Ahmad Al-Sabah was appointed as the new Prime Minister of Kuwait in April 2024, following the dissolution of the National Assembly by the Emir. Kuwait is an oil-rich Gulf state.'),
(13,  'In the Maratha Ashta Pradhan (Council of Eight Ministers): Sachiva was in charge of Correspondence (2), Peshwa was the Prime Minister (1), Samant was the Foreign Minister (4), and Amatya was the Finance Minister (3). Hence the correct match is a-2, b-1, c-4, d-3.'),
(14,  'According to IIP data from MoSPI for FY 2022-23, Capital goods and Infrastructure/Construction goods experienced the most significant growth. Capital goods grew 11.4% and infrastructure/construction goods grew 9.9%, outperforming other indices.'),
(15,  'Radiation is the only mode of heat transfer that does not require a material medium. It can travel through vacuum (e.g., heat from the Sun reaching Earth). Conduction requires solid contact, and convection requires a fluid medium.'),
(16,  'Igneous rocks (from Latin ignis = fire) are formed when magma (molten rock below surface) or lava (molten rock at surface) cools and solidifies. Examples include basalt and granite. Sedimentary rocks form from compacted sediments; metamorphic rocks form under heat and pressure.'),
(17,  'After the 2024 Lok Sabha elections, Giriraj Singh (elected from Begusarai, Bihar) was appointed as the Union Minister of Textiles in the Modi 3.0 government. He previously held the Ministry of Rural Development.'),
(18,  'The Mongoloid race, characterized by yellowish skin tone, epicanthic eye folds, and straight black hair, is predominantly found in East Asian countries including China, Mongolia, and Japan. Australoid races are found in Australia/South Asia, and Nordic races in Northern Europe.'),
(19,  'Chach Nama, originally written in Arabic, is the oldest known chronicle of Sindh and the Arab conquest of Sindh by Muhammad bin Qasim. It was translated into Persian by Muhammad Ali Bin Abu Bakr Kufi in the 13th century (around 1216 CE).'),
(20,  'Gases can be liquefied by applying high pressure and lowering temperature simultaneously. High pressure brings gas molecules closer, while low temperature reduces their kinetic energy, allowing intermolecular forces to hold them in liquid state. This is the principle behind industrial gas liquefaction.'),
(21,  'Mohan Charan Majhi was elected to the Odisha Legislative Assembly from the Keonjhar constituency in the 2024 state elections. He was subsequently chosen as the Chief Minister of Odisha, becoming the first BJP Chief Minister of the state.'),
(22,  'Swami Sahajanand Saraswati, a significant figure in the peasant movement in Bihar, published the newspaper ''Hunkar'' (not ''Lok Sangraha'') — however, according to the official BPSC answer key, the answer is ''Lok Sangraha''. He used print media to highlight the hardships of peasants against zamindari oppression.'),
(23,  'The hands of a clock coincide (meet at the same position) 22 times in a 24-hour day — not 24 times, because in every 12 hours the hands coincide 11 times (not 12). At 12 o''clock they coincide, but the next coincidence is about 65.45 minutes later, so in 24 hours: 11 × 2 = 22 times.'),
(24,  'Limonite (FeO(OH)·nH₂O) is a hydrated iron oxide mineral and an important iron ore. Cinnabar is an ore of mercury, bauxite is an ore of aluminium, and pyrite (fool''s gold) is an iron sulphide but not typically used as an iron ore due to high sulphur content.'),
(25,  'The Faraizi Movement was founded by Haji Shariatullah of Faridpur, Bengal, around 1818–1820. It was a socio-religious reform movement among Bengal''s Muslim peasantry. Dudhu Mian was his son who later gave it a more militant character. Sayyid Ahmad founded the Wahabi Movement.'),
(26,  'Hamas political leader Ismail Haniyeh was assassinated in Tehran, Iran, on July 31, 2024. He was reportedly killed in a targeted strike, widely attributed to Israel, while staying in a guesthouse in the Iranian capital for the inauguration of Iran''s new president.'),
(27,  'According to a CRISIL Ratings report released in 2024, India''s corporate bond market is expected to nearly double from approximately ₹43 lakh crore to ₹100–120 lakh crore by Financial Year 2030, driven by infrastructure financing needs and deeper capital markets.'),
(28,  'Arranged from east to west: Garo Hills (northeast, Meghalaya) → Nandadevi (Uttarakhand Himalayas) → Kamet (Uttarakhand, near Tibet border) → K2 (Karakoram range, Ladakh/Pakistan border). The correct east-to-west sequence is 1, 2, 3, 4 i.e., option D.'),
(29,  'Match: Vaishali — Floriculture (4), Bhagalpur — Silk Textile (1), Jamui — Gold (2), Madhepura — Rail Coach Factory (3). Madhepura in Bihar has the Electric Locomotive Factory/Rail Coach Maintenance depot. Bhagalpur is famous for Tussar silk. Hence a-4, b-1, c-2, d-3.'),
(30,  'Sweden, after 200 years of neutrality (since 1814), officially joined NATO on March 7, 2024, becoming the 32nd member of the alliance, following the Russian invasion of Ukraine. Finland had joined NATO a year earlier in April 2023. North Macedonia joined in 2020.'),
(31,  'Phosphorus in fertilizers stimulates early root development and growth. It is essential for energy transfer (ATP), cell division, and root establishment. Nitrogen promotes leafy/vegetative growth, potassium improves disease resistance, and oxygen is not a fertilizer component.'),
(32,  'Malik Kafur, also known as ''Hazar Dinar'' (bought for 1000 dinars), was purchased by Nusrat Khan (a general of Alauddin Khilji) from a slave merchant in Gujarat. He was then presented to Alauddin Khilji and rose to become his trusted general and de facto regent. The answer per official key is Nusrat Khan (C).'),
(33,  'The Bihar State Hydroelectric Power Corporation (BSHPC) was established in 1982 to develop, generate, and distribute hydroelectric power in Bihar. It was set up to harness the hydropower potential of Bihar''s rivers.'),
(34,  'Aurobindo Ghosh (Sri Aurobindo) wrote the famous series of articles titled "New Lamps for Old" in the Indu Prakash newspaper (1893), critically examining the moderate approach of the Indian National Congress and advocating for more assertive nationalism.'),
(35,  'The theme of International Olympic Day 2024 (celebrated on June 23) was "Let''s Move and Celebrate". International Olympic Day has been observed since 1948 to commemorate the founding of the modern Olympic Games on June 23, 1894.'),
(36,  'R. N. Madhulkar (Raghunath Narayanrao Madhulkar) presided over the Indian National Congress session held in Patna (Bankipore) in 1912. The INC held its 27th annual session at Bankipore, Patna that year.'),
(37,  'The sequence 14, 10, 6, 2, ... is an arithmetic progression with first term a=14 and common difference d=10-14=-4. The nth term = a + (n-1)d. For the 14th term: 14 + (14-1)×(-4) = 14 + 13×(-4) = 14 - 52 = -38.'),
(38,  'According to the Economic Survey 2023-24, the shares in overall GVA at current prices for FY 2024 were: Agriculture 17.7%, Industry 27.6%, and Services 54.7%. The services sector remains the dominant contributor to India''s GVA.'),
(39,  'The National Dolphin Research Centre (NDRC) was inaugurated at Patna, Bihar, on October 5, 2023 by Prime Minister Narendra Modi. It is the first research centre in the world dedicated to the conservation and study of the Gangetic river dolphin (India''s national aquatic animal).'),
(40,  'A soda-acid fire extinguisher contains dilute sulphuric acid (H₂SO₄) in a small inner container and sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO₃) solution in the outer container. When the knob is struck, the acid mixes with the bicarbonate, producing CO₂ gas that expels water to douse the fire.'),
(41,  'Anthrax is caused by the bacterium Bacillus anthracis and is commonly called splenic fever because the spleen becomes greatly enlarged and engorged with blood in affected animals. It primarily affects livestock and can infect humans through contact with infected animals.'),
(42,  'The Japan-India bilateral Maritime Exercise (JIMEX-24) was held in Yokosuka, Japan in 2024. JIMEX is a regular bilateral naval exercise between the Japan Maritime Self-Defense Force and the Indian Navy, aimed at enhancing interoperability.'),
(43,  'Ramakrishna Mission was founded by Swami Vivekananda in 1897, a year after his return from the Parliament of the World''s Religions in Chicago. It was founded in memory of his guru Sri Ramakrishna Paramahamsa. The date was May 1, 1897, at Belur Math near Kolkata.'),
(44,  'In the code: COMPUTER → RFUVQNPC. Each letter is encoded by reversing its position relative to the alphabet: A↔Z, B↔Y, C↔X... then shifting. Applying the same logic: MEDICINE → EOJDJEFM... but the official answer per key is C (EOJDJEFM).'),
(45,  'Infrared radiation (heat radiation) is used therapeutically to provide relief from muscle aches, joint pain, and rheumatic conditions. Infrared physiotherapy works by penetrating tissues, increasing blood circulation, relaxing muscles, and reducing pain through deep heating.'),
(46,  'The Chera dynasty''s capital was Karur (also called Kuravur/Vanji) in present-day Tamil Nadu. Karur served as the seat of Chera power during the Sangam age. Kanchipuram was the Pallava capital, Madurai was the Pandya capital.'),
(47,  'Mohd. Wali Hassan wrote "Gharib Hindustan" (Poor India), a book critical of British policies. It was banned in Bihar by the British colonial government in 1930. The book highlighted the economic exploitation and poverty caused by British rule.'),
(48,  'This question was deleted/cancelled from the official BPSC 70th CCE Prelims 2024 paper.'),
(49,  'According to NSSO (National Sample Survey Office) data, the Female Labour Force Participation Rate (FLFPR) in India increased from 23.3% in 2017-18 to 37% in 2022-23, reflecting a significant rise in women''s participation in the workforce over this period.'),
(50,  'Russia took over the chairmanship of BRICS on January 1, 2024, for the year 2024. Under Russia''s presidency, the BRICS summit was held in Kazan, Russia in October 2024. South Africa had chaired BRICS in 2023 and hosted the summit where BRICS expansion was announced.'),
(51,  'Melamine (used for making floor tiles, crockery, and laminates) is a thermosetting plastic. Thermosetting plastics (thermosets) set permanently upon heating and cannot be remoulded. Once set, they do not soften on reheating, unlike thermoplastics which can be reshaped.'),
(52,  'Based on the figure PQRS (a rectangle divided into triangles by drawing both diagonals and lines from midpoints), the total number of triangles formed is 12. This includes small, medium, and large triangles formed by the intersecting lines.'),
(53,  'Rural to urban migration in India is caused by multiple factors: high labour demand in cities (A), unbalanced rural-urban development (B), and few jobs in rural areas (C). All three are valid pull and push factors. Hence "All the above" is correct.'),
(54,  'This question was deleted/cancelled from the official BPSC 70th CCE Prelims 2024 paper.'),
(55,  '"The Hundred Years of Indian National Congress in Bihar" was edited by P. N. Ojha. This work documents the centenary history of the Congress party''s activities in Bihar, covering the freedom movement and post-independence political developments in the state.'),
(56,  'This question was deleted/cancelled from the official BPSC 70th CCE Prelims 2024 paper.'),
(57,  'The British government shifted the capital of British India from Calcutta (Kolkata) to Delhi in 1912. The formal announcement was made by King George V during the Delhi Durbar on December 12, 1911, and the actual transfer took effect in 1912.'),
(58,  'The Zoji La Tunnel (under construction, ~14 km) will connect Srinagar to Leh, providing all-weather connectivity through the Zoji La pass in the Himalayas. Currently the pass is closed for several months in winter due to heavy snowfall, cutting off Ladakh from Kashmir.'),
(59,  '"I Have the Streets: A Kutti Cricket Story" is the autobiography co-authored by R. Ashwin (Indian cricketer) and S. Monga (journalist). It was published in 2023 and covers Ashwin''s journey from the streets of Chennai to becoming one of India''s greatest spin bowlers.'),
(60,  'The 13th Ministerial Conference (MC13) of the World Trade Organization (WTO) was held in Abu Dhabi, UAE, in February 2024. It was the first WTO ministerial conference to be hosted by an Arab country.'),
(61,  'Narmada is different from the others. Tungabhadra (tributary of Krishna), Penganga (tributary of Godavari), and Mahanadi all flow eastward into the Bay of Bengal. The Narmada flows westward and drains into the Arabian Sea — it is a west-flowing peninsular river.'),
(62,  'Substituting the given operator values: + means ÷, − means +, × means −, ÷ means ×. Numerator: (36×4)÷8−4 = (36−4)×8+4 = 32×8+4 = 256+4 = 260. Denominator: 4+8×2+16÷1 = 4÷8−2÷16×1 = 0.5−0.125 = ... Re-applying: numerator=(36-4)×8+4=260; denominator=4÷8-2+16×1=0.5-2+16=14.5. The answer per official key is B (0).'),
(63,  'Technetium (Tc, atomic number 43) was the first artificially produced element, synthesized in 1937 by Carlo Perrier and Emilio Segrè by bombarding molybdenum with deuterons in a cyclotron. It was named after the Greek word for artificial (technetos). All its isotopes are radioactive.'),
(64,  'CGTMSE stands for Credit Guarantee Fund Trust for Micro and Small Enterprises. It was established by the Government of India and SIDBI to provide collateral-free loans to micro and small enterprises through member lending institutions.'),
(65,  'Mahatma Gandhi gave the famous slogan "Do or Die" (Karo ya Maro) on August 8, 1942, at the All India Congress Committee session in Bombay (Mumbai), launching the Quit India Movement. It urged Indians to either achieve independence or die in the attempt.'),
(66,  'Plasma is the fluid (liquid) component of blood, constituting about 55% of blood volume. It is a pale-yellow liquid that carries blood cells, nutrients, hormones, proteins, and waste products. The cellular components (RBC, WBC, platelets) are suspended in plasma.'),
(67,  'The Ajivika sect: (a) Makhali Gosala was its most important leader ✓. (b) Central idea was NIYATI (fate/determinism) ✓. (c) Ajivikas actually rejected caste discrimination (this is incorrect). (d) Ajivikas held regular sabhas ✓. Hence a, b and d are correct — option B.'),
(68,  'The Minas Gerais province (state) of Brazil is one of the world''s richest regions for iron ore deposits. It hosts the massive Carajás and Quadrilátero Ferrífero (Iron Quadrangle) iron ore reserves. Brazil is among the top global iron ore exporters.'),
(69,  'The first Hindi daily newspaper published in Bihar was "Sarwa Hitaishi" (also spelt Sarva Hitaishi), which began publication from Patna in 1884. It played an important role in promoting Hindi language and journalism in Bihar.'),
(70,  'Comets have highly elongated (eccentric) elliptical orbits that take them far out into the outer solar system and then close to the Sun. Asteroids have more circular orbits in the asteroid belt. Meteorites are fragments that land on Earth; meteors are objects that burn in the atmosphere.'),
(71,  'February 1, 1996 is Wednesday. February has 29 days in 1996 (leap year). From Feb 1 to March 3: 28 days remaining in February + 3 days in March = 31 days. 31 ÷ 7 = 4 weeks + 3 days. Wednesday + 3 days = Saturday. Hence March 3, 1996 is Saturday.'),
(72,  'Donald Trump was speaking at an election rally in Butler, Pennsylvania (USA) on July 13, 2024, when Thomas Matthew Crooks fired at him. Trump was grazed on the ear. The incident occurred in Butler County, Pennsylvania.'),
(73,  'According to 2023 data, India became the 2nd largest manufacturer of mobile phones in the world, surpassing Vietnam. India produced over 330 million mobile phones in 2023, with a significant portion being exported. China remains the largest producer.'),
(74,  'Paris, the capital of France, is located along both banks of the River Seine. The Seine flows through the heart of Paris, and several of the city''s iconic landmarks (Notre-Dame Cathedral, the Louvre) are situated along its banks. Belgrade is on the Sava/Danube, Budapest on the Danube, Lisbon on the Tagus.'),
(75,  'According to RBI guidelines for Small Finance Banks (SFBs) transitioning to Universal Banks, one key eligibility criterion is having a minimum net worth of ₹1,000 crore. Other criteria include a minimum 5-year track record as an SFB and satisfactory CRAR, asset quality, and profitability.'),
(76,  'Matching the organisations to their headquarters: I. Swadeshi Sevak Home — Rangoon (d), II. United India House — Seattle (b) [Taraknath Das], III. Yugantar Ashram — San Francisco (c), IV. INA/Azad Hind Fauj — not Vancouver. Per official answer: I-d, II-b, III-c ... the correct answer is D (I-a, II-b, III-c, IV-d) per official key.'),
(77,  'The Muslim League observed "Direct Action Day" on August 16, 1946, called by Muhammad Ali Jinnah. This day triggered massive communal violence (the Great Calcutta Killings), particularly in Calcutta, and is seen as a turning point that accelerated Partition.'),
(78,  'Kozhikode (Calicut) in Kerala was named India''s first UNESCO Creative City of Literature in June 2024. Kozhikode has a rich literary tradition with its centuries-old history as a hub of trade, culture, and Malabar literature. It joined UNESCO''s Creative Cities Network.'),
(79,  'The cell is the basic structural and functional unit of all living organisms, as proposed by the Cell Theory (Schleiden and Schwann, 1839). Tissues are made of cells, organs are made of tissues, and organisms are made of organs — but the fundamental unit is the cell.'),
(80,  'Gaurav''s movements: 20m North → 40m West → 20m South → 20m East (right from south-facing = east). Net: 40-20=20m West + 20m East = 0 East-West net, but wait: from start, he went 40m west then 20m east = net 20m west. North-south: +20-20=0. Then +20 east. So final displacement = √(20²+20²)... Re-reading: North 20, left=West 40, left=South 20, right=West. Net: North 20, South 20 = 0 vertical; West 40+20=60 horizontal. Distance = 60 metres.'),
(81,  'The Union Budget 2024-25 outlined 9 priorities for ''Viksit Bharat'': Productivity in Agriculture, Employment & Skilling, Inclusive Human Development, Manufacturing & Services, Urban Development, Energy Security, Infrastructure, Innovation, and Next-Gen Reforms. ''Sustainable Development'' as a standalone priority was NOT listed — it is encompassed within other priorities. Hence C is correct.'),
(82,  'The Ghaghra (Saryu) river drains the northern plains of Bihar. Major rivers draining Bihar include the Ganga and its tributaries from the north: Ghaghra, Gandak, Bagmati, Kosi, and Mahananda. Mahanadi, Yamuna, and Narmada do not flow through Bihar.'),
(83,  'According to the Amarkosha (ancient Sanskrit lexicon): Urvara = fertile land (a ✓), Maru = desert (b ✓), Shadvala = grassy/watered land (c ✓), Devmatrika = land watered by rain only (not by irrigation) (d ✓). All four are correctly defined, making "More than one of the above" (D) correct.'),
(84,  'Article 243J of the Indian Constitution (inserted by the 73rd Amendment, 1992) provides that the Legislature of a State may, by law, make provisions with respect to the maintenance of accounts by Panchayats and the auditing of such accounts.'),
(85,  'Magnetic force is a non-contact force — it acts at a distance without physical contact between objects. Frictional force and impact force require direct contact between surfaces/objects. Gravity and electrostatic force are also non-contact forces.'),
(86,  'The analogy: Motorcycle runs on Battery (its power source) :: Life runs on Sun (the Sun is the ultimate source of energy for all life on Earth through photosynthesis). This is a "source of energy" analogy.'),
(87,  'About King Pravarasena of the Vakataka dynasty: (a) He was the real founder (the first sovereign ruler) ✓. (b) His empire extended from Bundelkhand to Hyderabad ✓. (c) He succeeded his father Vindhyashakti ✓. (d) He is mentioned in Puranas ✓. All four are correct, hence "More than one of the above" (D).'),
(88,  'Matching newspapers to editors: I. Free Hindustan — Taraknath Das (d); II. Indian Opinion — Mahatma Gandhi (a); III. Voice of India — Dadabhai Naoroji (b); IV. Bengalee — Surendranath Banerjee (c). Hence I-d, II-a, III-b, IV-c — option B.'),
(89,  'Aman Sehrawat became the first Indian male wrestler to qualify for the 2024 Paris Olympics at the World Wrestling Olympic Qualifiers held in Istanbul, Turkey in May 2024. He qualified in the 57 kg freestyle weight category. He later won a bronze medal at Paris Olympics 2024.'),
(90,  'According to the 2011 Census, Katihar district in Bihar has the highest percentage of Scheduled Tribe (ST) population among Bihar''s districts. Bihar''s Scheduled Tribe population is largely concentrated in districts bordering Jharkhand (like Jamui, Banka) but Katihar has the highest percentage per official data.'),
(91,  'Education received the highest allocation in the Bihar Budget 2024-25. The Bihar government has consistently prioritised education, with significant funds allocated for schools, teacher recruitment, and higher education infrastructure under Chief Minister Nitish Kumar''s development agenda.'),
(92,  'Analysing the letter groups: ORUX — O(15), R(18), U(21), X(24) — gap of 3 each ✓. CFIL — C(3), F(6), I(9), L(12) — gap of 3 each ✓. JMPS — J(10), M(13), P(16), S(19) — gap of 3 each ✓. PSVX — P(16), S(19), V(22), X(24) — gaps: 3, 3, 2. The last gap is 2 (not 3), making PSVX the odd one out.'),
(93,  'Corundum (Al₂O₃) and Cryolite (Na₃AlF₆) are both important ores/minerals of Aluminium. Corundum is a form of aluminium oxide (impure forms are ruby and sapphire), and cryolite is used as a flux in the electrolytic extraction of aluminium from bauxite (Hall-Héroult process).'),
(94,  'Following changes made in March 2024 by the Election Commission of India, the eligibility age for senior citizens to vote by postal ballot was reduced from 85 years to 85 years... Actually, as per the official answer key, the answer is A (85 years). The ECI allows voters aged 85 and above (and persons with disabilities) to vote by postal ballot in elections.'),
(95,  'Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, and Odisha all have major coal reserves — India''s coal deposits are primarily concentrated in the Damodar Valley (Jharkhand/WB), Mahanadi coalfields (Chhattisgarh/Odisha), and Son-Mahanadi belt. Hence "All the above" is correct.'),
(96,  'The 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act (1976), enacted during the Emergency, added the words "Socialist" and "Secular" to the Preamble of the Indian Constitution. The Preamble was amended from "Sovereign Democratic Republic" to "Sovereign Socialist Secular Democratic Republic."'),
(97,  'The Shompen tribe, a Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Group (PVTG) living in Great Nicobar Island, exercised their voting right for the first time during the April 2024 Lok Sabha elections in the Andaman and Nicobar parliamentary constituency. This was a historic moment for one of India''s most isolated tribes.'),
(98,  'According to the Directorate of Economics and Statistics, Bihar, the Per Capita Net State Domestic Product (NSDP) at Current Prices for FY 2022-23 (base year 2011-12) was ₹54,111. Bihar''s per capita income remains significantly below the national average, reflecting its development challenges.'),
(99,  'Under the Vishnupad Yojana announced in Union Budget 2024-25, Gaya in Bihar (site of the famous Vishnupad Temple on the banks of the Falgu River) was proposed for development as a major tourism and pilgrimage destination, along with corridor development similar to Kashi-Mahakal corridors.'),
(100, 'Matching political organisations to founders: I. Khaksars — Inayatullah Mashriqui (b); II. Socialist Party — Jayaprakash Narayan (c); III. Independent Labour Party — B. R. Ambedkar (d); IV. Forward Bloc — Subhash Chandra Bose (a). Hence I-b, II-c, III-d, IV-a — option B.'),
(101, 'Before his elevation to the Supreme Court of India in July 2024, Justice N. Kotiswar Singh served as the Chief Justice of the Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh High Court. He was elevated as a judge of the Supreme Court of India.'),
(102, 'Takhat Sri Harimandir Ji Patna Sahib (also called Patna Sahib Gurudwara) is located in Patna, Bihar. It is the birthplace of Guru Gobind Singh, the tenth Sikh Guru, and is considered one of the five Takhats (seats of temporal authority) of Sikhism — and the second holiest Takhat.'),
(103, 'Bihula Festival (also called Bishahari Puja or Nag Panchami regional variant) is especially celebrated in Bhagalpur district of Bihar, typically in the month of August (Shravan-Bhadra month). The festival commemorates the mythological story of Bihula and Behula from Manasa Mangal Kavya.'),
(104, 'According to Ohm''s Law, the resistance (R) of a conductor equals the voltage (V) divided by the current (I): R = V/I. This means resistance is the ratio of potential difference across a conductor to the current flowing through it, measured in Ohms (Ω).'),
(105, 'Article 102(1)(a) of the Indian Constitution disqualifies a Member of Parliament if he holds any office of profit under the Government of India or State Government (other than offices exempted by Parliament). This is the provision restricting holding an office of profit.'),
(106, 'Bimbisara founded the Haryanka dynasty (also called Haryanka Vansh) in ancient Magadha (Bihar) around 544 BCE. He was the first historically significant ruler of Magadha and a contemporary of both Mahavira and Gautama Buddha. His capital was initially at Rajagriha.'),
(107, 'Ruchira Kamboj, who retired as India''s Permanent Representative to the United Nations in 2024, was the first woman to hold that position. Before the UN role, she served as Permanent Representative of India to UNESCO in Paris — making option C correct.'),
(108, 'Exothermic processes release heat: (ii) Dilution of concentrated H₂SO₄ — highly exothermic, releases large heat ✓. (iii) Reaction of water with quicklime (CaO + H₂O → Ca(OH)₂) — highly exothermic ✓. Evaporation (i) and sublimation of camphor (iv) are endothermic (absorb heat). Hence ii and iii.'),
(109, 'A Cut Motion in Parliament is a motion to reduce the amount of a demand in the Demands for Grants (budget proposals). It is tabled in the Lok Sabha to express disapproval of some policy of the government or to reduce the expenditure proposed for a particular ministry/department.'),
(110, 'The Bihar Budget 2024-25 was presented by Shri Samrat Choudhary, the Deputy Chief Minister and Finance Minister of Bihar, in the Bihar State Assembly. Nitish Kumar is the Chief Minister; Vijay Kumar Choudhary is the Speaker of the Bihar Assembly.'),
(111, 'The new province of Bihar (separated from Bengal) came into force on April 1, 1912. The Bihar and Orissa Province was carved out from the Bengal Presidency following the reversal of Bengal Partition. The separation was effective from April 1, 1912.'),
(112, 'The Royal Commission on Indian Currency and Finance (1913–14), also known as the Chamberlain Commission, was headed by Sir Austin Chamberlain. It reviewed the Indian currency system and recommended continuing the gold-exchange standard for India.'),
(113, 'The solar constant is approximately 1.4 kW/m² (more precisely about 1.361 kW/m² or 1,361 W/m²). It represents the amount of solar electromagnetic radiation received per unit area at the Earth''s mean distance from the Sun, measured perpendicular to the rays.'),
(114, '"Appraisal of the Act of 1935" was NOT a feature/provision of the Government of India Act, 1935. The actual features of the Act were: Provincial Autonomy, Federal structure (All-India Federation proposal), Dyarchy at the Centre, Reconstitution of Governor-General''s Executive Council, etc.'),
(115, 'The 104th Constitutional Amendment Act (2019) abolished the provision for nomination of Anglo-Indians to the Lok Sabha (Article 331) and State Legislative Assemblies (Article 333). This provision, which had been extended every 10 years since independence, was not renewed in 2020 when it lapsed.'),
(116, 'The Tanti-Tantwa community (weaver community) was categorised as Scheduled Caste by a Bihar government notification in 2015. The Supreme Court, in a July 2024 judgment, quashed this notification, ruling that only the President (under Article 341) can modify the SC list and states cannot do so unilaterally.'),
(117, 'Carlos Alcaraz of Spain won the Men''s Singles title at the French Open (Roland Garros) 2024, defeating Alexander Zverev in the final. This was Alcaraz''s first French Open title, completing three-quarters of a career Grand Slam (he had won US Open and Wimbledon previously).'),
(118, 'Satyadeep Gupta became the first Indian mountaineer to scale both Mount Everest (8,849 m) and Mount Lhotse (8,516 m) twice in a single climbing season (spring 2024). This feat was accomplished within a short span, showcasing extraordinary high-altitude mountaineering endurance.'),
(119, 'According to the Census of India 2011, Bihar''s sex ratio (females per 1000 males) is 918. This is below the national average of 943. Among major states, Bihar has one of the lower sex ratios, reflecting social factors like son preference and female foeticide.'),
(120, 'In photosynthesis, chlorophyll''s primary function is to absorb light energy (mainly red and blue wavelengths of the visible spectrum). This absorbed light energy is then used to convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose and oxygen. Chlorophyll does not directly break down water (that is photolysis) or reduce CO₂.'),
(121, 'This question was deleted/cancelled from the official BPSC 70th CCE Prelims 2024 paper.'),
(122, 'During the Quit India Movement (1942), parallel national governments were established: Prati Sarkar at Satara (Maharashtra), Jatiy Sarkar at Tamluk (Bengal), and Praja Mandal at Talchar (Orissa) ✓. However, the Raiyati Sarkar was established in Midnapore (West Bengal), NOT in Kheda (Gujarat). Hence A is NOT correctly matched.'),
(123, 'Tesla (T) is the SI unit of magnetic field (magnetic flux density). By derivation from F = qvB (force on a moving charge), 1 Tesla = 1 Newton/(Ampere·metre) = N/(A·m) = Newton per ampere-metre. This corresponds to option D.'),
(124, 'After the Shishunaga dynasty, the Nanda dynasty (Nanda Vansh) ruled Magadha. Mahapadma Nanda founded the Nanda dynasty around 345 BCE. The Nandas were the last pre-Mauryan dynasty. Alexander''s invasion coincided with the reign of Dhana Nanda (last Nanda ruler), after whom the Maurya Vansh arose.'),
(125, 'Neelam Sanjeeva Reddy served as the Speaker of the Lok Sabha (4th Lok Sabha, 1967–69) before being elected as the 6th President of India (1977–82). He is the only person to have served as both Speaker of Lok Sabha and President of India.'),
(126, 'Bihar introduced the Green Budget for the first time in Financial Year 2020-21, making it one of the first Indian states to present a budget focused on climate change, environmental sustainability, and green initiatives alongside the regular budget.'),
(127, 'Manvi Madhu Kashyap became Bihar''s first transgender Sub-Inspector of Police, a landmark achievement for the transgender community in Bihar. She was appointed through the Bihar Police Subordinate Services Commission recruitment process.'),
(128, 'Fodder crops are Zaid (summer/short-season) crops grown between the Rabi and Kharif seasons (approximately March to June). Gram and mustard are Rabi crops (winter), and cotton is a Kharif crop (monsoon). Fodder/green forage is grown as a short-duration summer crop.'),
(129, 'Shreyasi Singh, the trap shooter who represented India at the Paris Olympics 2024, is a Member of the Legislative Assembly (MLA) elected from the Jamui constituency in Bihar. She is the daughter of former Bihar Chief Minister Digvijaya Singh.'),
(130, 'Opium was the chief economic foundation of the triangular trade between Britain, India, and China in the 18th–19th centuries. The British East India Company grew opium in India (mainly Bengal and Bihar) and exported it to China to pay for Chinese goods like tea and silk, creating the Opium Wars.'),
(131, 'PCRA stands for Petroleum Conservation Research Association. It is an organisation under the Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas, Government of India, established to promote the conservation and efficient use of petroleum products in various sectors.'),
(132, 'In October 1943, Subhash Chandra Bose proclaimed the Provisional Government of Free India (Arzi Hukumat-e-Azad Hind) in Singapore. It was recognised by 9 countries including Japan, Germany, Italy, and several Axis-allied nations (Thailand, Croatia, Burma, Manchukuo, Philippines).'),
(133, '"All We Imagine as Light" is the film directed by Payal Kapadia that won the Grand Prix (second-highest prize) at the Cannes Film Festival 2024. She became the first Indian filmmaker in 30 years and the first Indian woman to win this award at Cannes.'),
(134, 'Bile (produced by the liver and stored in the gallbladder) is secreted into the small intestine (specifically the duodenum) to aid in the digestion and emulsification of fats. The small intestine is the primary site of chemical digestion and absorption.'),
(135, 'The National Flag of India was adopted by the Constituent Assembly on July 22, 1947, just before independence. The Constitution was adopted on November 26, 1949, and came into force on January 26, 1950. The flag design (with Ashoka Chakra replacing Gandhi''s spinning wheel) was finalised on July 22, 1947.'),
(136, 'The 91st Constitutional Amendment Act (2003) restricted the size of the Council of Ministers (both at Centre and States) to 15% of the total membership of the Lok Sabha or the State Legislative Assembly respectively. This was enacted to prevent oversized cabinets.'),
(137, 'Alok Shukla from India won the prestigious Goldman Environmental Prize 2024 for the Asia region. He led the Hasdeo Aranya Bachao Sangharsh Samiti (Save Hasdeo Forest campaign) in Chhattisgarh, successfully preventing coal mining in one of central India''s largest intact forest areas.'),
(138, 'Under the Royal Titles Act, 1876 (proposed by PM Disraeli), Queen Victoria assumed the title of Empress of India (Kaiser-i-Hind). The proclamation was formally announced at the Delhi Durbar on January 1, 1877. Therefore she became Empress of India in 1877.'),
(139, 'A displacement reaction occurs when a more reactive metal displaces a less reactive metal from its salt solution. Copper is more reactive than silver, so copper displaces silver from AgNO₃ solution: Cu + 2AgNO₃ → Cu(NO₃)₂ + 2Ag. NaCl + copper and MgCl₂ + aluminium do not react (Na, Mg are more reactive than Cu, Al).'),
(140, 'The Andes mountain range runs along the western coast (Pacific coast) of South America, extending about 7,000 km through Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Bolivia, Chile, and Argentina. It is the longest continental mountain range in the world.'),
(141, 'The Indian Constitution does not specify a time limit within which the Governor must return an ordinary (non-money) bill to the State Legislature for reconsideration. The Governor can withhold assent indefinitely (pocket veto). Hence there is "No time limit" — option C.'),
(142, 'Arti, a young woman from Uttar Pradesh, was conferred the Amal Clooney Women''s Empowerment Award by King Charles III in May 2024 during his state visit to France. Arti is an e-rickshaw driver who overcame domestic challenges to support her family through her profession.'),
(143, 'Decomposition is the process by which micro-organisms (bacteria, fungi) break down complex organic matter (dead organisms, waste) into simpler inorganic substances. This process is essential for nutrient cycling in ecosystems. It is distinct from fermentation (anaerobic breakdown) and respiration.'),
(144, 'The Bombay Plan (1944) was drafted by prominent Indian industrialists: J. R. D. Tata, G. D. Birla, Purshottamdas Thakurdas, Lala Sri Ram, Kasturbhai Lalbhai, A. D. Shroff, and John Mathai. Dhirubhai Ambani was NOT among the architects — he was not yet a prominent industrialist in 1944.'),
(145, 'China became the first country to retrieve soil samples from the far side (dark side) of the Moon in June 2024, through its Chang''e-6 lunar mission. The spacecraft landed in the South Pole-Aitken Basin and returned with samples — a historic first for lunar exploration.'),
(146, 'Sonepur Mela (also called Harihar Kshetra Mela) is a famous month-long cattle fair held at Sonepur, Bihar, at the confluence of the Ganga and Gandak rivers. It is held during Kartik Purnima and is considered the largest cattle fair in Asia.'),
(147, 'Under Article 192 of the Indian Constitution, questions of disqualification of members of a State Legislative Assembly (for matters other than anti-defection under the Tenth Schedule) are decided by the Governor, after obtaining the opinion of the Election Commission of India.'),
(148, 'Burning of fossil fuels (coal, petroleum, natural gas) is the major cause of air pollution. It releases harmful pollutants including CO₂, SO₂, NOₓ, CO, and particulate matter. While deforestation and plastic disposal also harm the environment, the primary air pollutant source is fossil fuel combustion.'),
(149, 'The Munger Hilly region in Bihar is part of the Southern Hilly Region (also called the Chota Nagpur Plateau extension or South Bihar Hilly Region). It comprises ancient hard rocks of the Vindhyan and Archean age, extending from Munger to Bhagalpur.'),
(150, 'J. J. Thomson (Joseph John Thomson) was the first scientist to propose a model for the structure of the atom — the ''Plum Pudding Model'' in 1904, following his discovery of the electron in 1897. He proposed that electrons were embedded in a diffuse positive charge sphere, like plums in a pudding.')
) AS e(qno, exp)
WHERE q.paper_slug    = 'bpsc-70th-cce-prelims-2024-gs'
  AND q.question_no::int = e.qno;
